# One paragraph per section and subsection, each sentence grounded in the nodes it stands for. Status ai-draft.
BRIEF = {
    "section-1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-1-p1">At high energies quantum gravity is black holes, but weakly coupled strings have an intermediate regime of highly excited strings with Hagedorn growth, and whether a black hole in the last stages of evaporation becomes a self-gravitating string state, a string star, has only been argued qualitatively because the two phases have no reliable overlap.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p2">The paper follows both phases to the $\tfrac12$-BPS index, reached from the thermal partition function by $\beta\Omega=2\pi i$; the index is known exactly and is independent of temperature and coupling, so agreement with the microscopic answer tests the transition itself.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p3">The string star is the Horowitz–Polchinski solution of the Atick–Witten effective theory for the thermal winding scalar, reliable only for $R-R_H\ll1$ and small $\Omega$; at $\Omega\sim O(1)$ the thermal scalar's potential is string-sized and the saddle cannot be followed.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p4">Yet a new effective theory exists near the index for heterotic strings: at the self-dual radius the theory is the half-maximal $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$ gauged supergravity, Higgsed away from $R=1$, the winding mode is its W boson, and turning on $\nu$ is a magnetic field that makes the W tachyonic, the Ambjørn–Olesen phenomenon.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p5 section-1-p6 section-1-p7">The paper then finds W stars, bubbles of W condensate that decouple as $\nu\to0$ like rotating black holes; adds charges, where charged W stars approach the microscopic index while both charged phases decouple at the index, in tension with a BPS small black hole; treats type II, where a tower of windings becomes light and the effective theory is the T-dual $(d+1)$-dimensional supergravity; and raises a puzzle for bosonic strings.</span></p>""",
    "sec-het_s1": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-setup">The twisted partition function $Z(R,\nu)=\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-2\pi R\hat H-2\pi i\nu J}(-1)^F$, which is the index at $\nu=0$ and the thermal partition function at $\nu=1$, is flat space with a twisted identification; in Kaluza–Klein form it is the Euclidean magnetic Melvin background, with a graviphoton localised near the rotation axis.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet">On the worldsheet the thermal winding scalar, tachyonic below the Hagedorn radius, flows as $\nu\to0$ into a massive complex vector of mass $(R-1/R)^2$, massless at the self-dual radius where the heterotic gauge group is enhanced to $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$; for small $\nu$ the ground state is tachyonic again below $R_H(\nu)=1+\sqrt{\nu/2}+\dots$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-su2_sugra">The corresponding low-energy theory, the 9d half-maximal $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$ gauged supergravity, is written to all orders in the fields at two derivatives; in unitary gauge the W boson mass $2\cosh\sigma-2=(R-1/R)^2$ reproduces the worldsheet.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum">On the Melvin background the W spectrum is Landau levels that match the worldsheet exactly, and the Zeeman coupling makes the longitudinal ground state the lightest, with $m^2=(R-1/R)^2-2\nu/R^2$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-electroweak">This is the electroweak Ambjørn–Olesen instability of W bosons in a strong magnetic field, except that the Melvin background lifts the Landau degeneracy so that a single mode condenses.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-setup": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-setup-p1">The object of study is $Z(R,\nu)=\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-2\pi R\hat H-2\pi i\nu J}(-1)^F$ with $J$ a rotation generator: $\nu=0$ is the supersymmetric index and, because $(-1)^F=e^{2\pi iJ}$, $\nu=1$ is the thermal partition function.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-setup-p2">It is the partition function on flat space with the identification $(x^0,\theta')\sim(x^0+2\pi,\theta'+2\pi\nu)$ and periodic fermions.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-setup-p3">In Kaluza–Klein variables, with left and right vectors $A=a+b$, $\bar A=a-b$ and the shifted angle $\theta=\theta'-\nu x^0$, the background has a non-trivial 9d metric, dilaton and graviphoton $A=\bar A$: the Euclidean magnetic Melvin background, a magnetic field localised near $\rho=0$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-worldsheet": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p1">The worldsheet theory is the flat-space theory orbifolded by a shift of $X^0$ combined with the rotation $Z\mapsto e^{2\pi i\nu}Z$, $\Psi\mapsto e^{2\pi i\nu}\Psi$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p2">In the $w=1$ sector the mass-shell condition gives, at $\nu=1$, the Atick–Witten thermal scalar, tachyonic for $R<R_H=\tfrac12(2+\sqrt2)$ and arbitrarily light just above it.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p3">Away from $\nu=1$ the twisted boundary conditions shift the oscillator frequencies and open string-scale gaps $\Delta m^2\sim|1-\nu|$, so the Atick–Witten theory is only meaningful near the thermal point.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p4">At $\nu=0$ the spectrum is dense again: the winding ground state has $\tilde N=\tfrac12$, mass $(R-1/R)^2$ and thermal momentum $n=1$, and since the same level is reached with any worldsheet fermion it is a massive complex vector.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p5">Its mass is non-negative for all $R$, as the index demands, and vanishes at $R=1$, where $U(1)_L\times U(1)_R$ is enhanced to $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$: the massless spectrum is the 9d gravity multiplet, the $U(1)_R$ vector, an $SU(2)_L$ gauge field and an adjoint Higgs with the radion as $S^3$, and away from $R=1$ the broken gauge fields and scalars form the complex massive W boson, which is the winding mode.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p6">For small $\nu$ the light levels are $N=k\nu$, $\tilde N=\tfrac12+\nu\tilde k$ with spacing $\Delta m^2\sim\nu$, so the EFT keeps its scale separation; the ground state has $m^2=(R-1/R)^2-2\nu/R^2+O(\nu^2)$ and is tachyonic for $R<R_H(\nu)$, $R_H^2(\nu)=1+\sqrt{2\nu}+O(\nu)$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-worldsheet-p7">In the new theory W stands for winding, and $R-1\ll1$, $\nu\ll1$ are a small Higgs VEV and a small magnetic field.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-su2_sugra": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-su2_sugra-p1">Reducing the heterotic action on the thermal circle and changing to the left/right vector basis gives the Kaluza–Klein theory (2.19) with $\cosh\sigma$ and $\sinh\sigma$ gauge couplings.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-su2_sugra-p2">Since the reduction keeps all 16 supercharges, the theory is half-maximal 9d supergravity; the winding mode is charged under $A$ only, so two new vector multiplets combine with $A,\sigma$ into an $SU(2)$ vector multiplet, and appendix A fixes the bosonic action (2.20), which has no potential for the adjoint scalar.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-su2_sugra-p3">In unitary gauge $S=\sigma T^3$, with $W=(A^1+iA^2)/\sqrt2$ of charge $+1$ under $U(1)_L$, the action (2.24) is the KK theory plus a massive charged vector with a magnetic moment coupling; this is the first all-orders two-derivative form of the theory.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-su2_sugra-p4">On $\mathbb R^d\times S^1_R$ the radius is a Higgs VEV, $\sigma,A,\bar A$ stay massless and $m_W^2=2\cosh\sigma-2=(R-1/R)^2$ matches the worldsheet; the EFT is exact in the fields but needs $R-1\ll1$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-mag_spectrum": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum-p1">The index background is supersymmetric with a non-negative spectrum, yet for $\nu>0$ the winding spectrum is tachyonic; the goal is to see this in the W boson spectrum on the Melvin background, whose quadratic action (2.28) contains a non-positive magnetic dipole term.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum-p2">Transverse polarisations behave as a charged scalar with a position-dependent mass, as the thermal scalar does near $\nu=1$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum-p3">Expanding to $O(\nu)$ the problem is a scalar in the constant field $\hat F=i\nu\,dz\wedge d\bar z$ plus an orbital angular momentum term; Landau levels $w_{k,\tilde k}$ built from two oscillator pairs diagonalise it, with eigenvalues $\lambda_{k,\tilde k}$ (2.40) that reproduce the worldsheet (2.14), and the ground state is a Gaussian of size $|z|^2\sim1/\nu$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum-p4">Longitudinal polarisations $w_\pm$ mix with a pure-gauge transverse mode; using the gauge constraint the action diagonalises to $\lambda^{(\pm)}_{k,\tilde k}=\lambda_{k,\tilde k\pm1}$, again matching the worldsheet, and the ground state $w_-=w_{0,0}$ has $m^2=(R-1/R)^2-2\nu/R^2$, the target-space origin of the Hagedorn instability.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-mag_spectrum-p5">The W boson sees a magnetic field, the Zeeman splitting allows a longitudinal tachyon, and a second term lifts the degeneracy to a unique ground state.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-electroweak": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-electroweak-p1">The theory resembles the electroweak model with the same gauge group but a triplet Higgs, so it breaks to $U(1)\times U(1)$ with one W; the quadratic W action (2.52) is the same in both.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-electroweak-p2">A charged spin-$j$ particle in a constant field has $E^2=m^2+qB(1+2k+2s)$; a scalar is gapped, but the W with $s=-1$ has $E^2_{0,-1}=m^2-qB$ and turns tachyonic at high field, the Ambjørn–Olesen phenomenon.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-electroweak-p3">In electroweak theory the huge Landau degeneracy governs the physics above the critical field; the Melvin background breaks translations and lifts it, so just below the critical $\nu$ only $w_-=w_{0,0}$ condenses.</span></p>""",
    "sec-w_stars": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-w_stars-p1 sec-w_stars-p2">Horowitz and Polchinski found a self-gravitating condensate of the light thermal scalar for $2<d<6$, proposed as the stringy description of hot black holes; near the index the analogue is a normalisable W boson saddle of the $d$-dimensional $SU(2)$ supergravity on the Melvin background.</span> <span data-covers="sec-w_stars-p3 sec-w_stars-p4">At $\nu=0$ the Lagrangian is positive and has no such saddles, so $\nu$ is kept finite and the saddle is a metastable bubble of W condensate; “string star” is the collective term, “W star” the heterotic $SU(2)$ one.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-low_e_eft">Expanding the action to cubic order near $R_H(\nu)$, the W sources the Newtonian potential $\varphi$ and the KK vector $a$ with coupling $\sqrt{2\nu}$; integrating them out gives Newtonian attraction between $|W|^2$ densities and an Ampère force between winding currents.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT">When the winding ground state is lighter than the Landau gap, $m^2\ll\nu$, only the Landau ground state matters and the theory becomes $(d-2)$-dimensional with a marginal, log-regulated kernel; a variational solution exists for $2<d<6$ with $\chi_0^2\sim m^2/(\nu\log(\nu/m^2))$ and $L\sim1/m$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop">The W star has $I\sim m^{6-d}/(\nu\log(\nu/m^2))$, obeys $S_\nu=2\pi l_sE$, transitions to free strings at $I\sim1$, lies on one line with Horowitz–Polchinski as $\nu$ varies, and as $\nu\to0$ decouples with diverging action and amplitude.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans">Myers–Perry black holes at fixed temperature also decouple as $\nu\to0$, with $r_h\sim R/\nu$ and diverging mass and spin for $D>4$; extrapolated to intermediate temperatures, the two saddles agree in their $\nu$ divergences for $D>4$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-low_e_eft": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-low_e_eft-p1">The temperature is tuned so that the winding ground state is light, $R-R_H(\nu)\ll1$ with $\nu\ll1$, which also keeps the amplitude small.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-low_e_eft-p2">Expanding the $SU(2)$ action in $\varphi=\sigma-\sigma_0$, $a$, $\bar a$ and $W$ gives the quadratic W action (2.28) plus cubic couplings; with $\partial\sim\nu^{1/2}$ the two-derivative cubic terms and the metric and dilaton couplings are subleading.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-low_e_eft-p3">At $R=R_H(\nu)$, where $R^2-R^{-2}=2\sqrt{2\nu}$, $\bar a$ decouples and the cubic action (3.8) is $\sqrt{2\nu}(|W|^2\varphi+a_jJ^j)$ with the winding current $J$: like Horowitz–Polchinski, but with a vector and with both a Newtonian and a Kaluza–Klein coupling.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-low_e_eft-p4">Integrating out $\varphi$ and $a$ gives a negative-definite gravitational self-energy and an Ampère term of undetermined sign; local repulsive terms, positive by supersymmetry, remain subleading for large solutions.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-lower_E_EFT": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT-p1">Tuning $R-R_H(\nu)\ll\sqrt\nu$ makes the winding ground state much lighter than the Landau gap, so only its $(d-2)$-dimensional momentum modes are light.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT-p2">With $W=W_0(z,\bar z)\chi(x)$ and the Gaussian vortex $W_0$ of size $1/\nu$, the action becomes the $(d-2)$-dimensional theory (3.16) for $\chi$ with a kernel $G(r)$ that is numerically positive, hence attractive.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT-p3">In $d-2$ dimensions the Newtonian force is marginal and logarithmically divergent, so the vortex size must be kept as a UV scale, $G(r)\approx C_d/(r^2+c/\nu)^{(d-2)/2}$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT-p4">A variational ansatz $\chi=\chi_0f(|x|/L)$ gives $m^2L^2\approx\frac{6-d}{d-2}\frac{I_1}{I_2}$, so solutions exist for $2<d<6$, with $\chi_0^2\sim m^2/(\nu\log(\nu/m^2))$ and $L\sim1/m$, self-consistently small and large; numerics with the approximate kernel agree.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-lower_E_EFT-p5">The neglected quartic coupling is subdominant thanks to the logarithmic enhancement.</span></p>""",
    "sec-W_star_prop": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop-p1">The on-shell action is $I\sim m^{6-d}/(\nu\log(\nu/m^2))$, the energy $E\sim m^{4-d}/(\sqrt\nu\log)$, and $S_\nu\equiv S+\beta\Omega J$ obeys $S_\nu=2\pi l_sE$: Hagedorn growth persists next to an index with no Hagedorn temperature.</span> <span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop-p2">Quantum effects take over at $I\sim1$, giving the regime of validity (3.28) and the transition energy (3.29).</span> <span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop-p3">The scalings match Horowitz–Polchinski up to the log, and since the low-energy limit exists for every $0<\nu<1$, the two solutions presumably lie on one line in $\nu$ as long as the force stays attractive.</span> <span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop-p4">At fixed $m^2$ and $\nu\to0$ the size is $L\sim1/\sqrt\nu$, the action $I\sim1/\nu$, and the effective potential $m^2\chi_0^2-\nu\chi_0^4$ has its metastable minimum at $\chi_0^2\sim m^2/\nu$: the W star does not dissolve but decouples with diverging free energy and amplitude.</span> <span data-covers="sec-W_star_prop-p5">At fixed energy it presumably transitions to free strings, with a transition scale that diverges at $\nu=0$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-bh_w_trans": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p1">Horowitz–Polchinski roughly matches black holes at $R\sim l_s$ and was extended to small angular velocity; the W star is its deformation to $\nu\sim0$, to be compared with rotating black holes there.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p2 subsec-bh_w_trans-p3">Euclidean Myers–Perry holes with $a=ib$ have mass, entropy and spin (3.31), temperature, angular velocity and twist (3.33–3.34), and the Smarr relation $S_\nu/(2\pi R)=\frac{D-3}{D-2}M$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p4">At fixed temperature the $\nu\to0$ limit is singular: for $D>4$, $r_h,b\sim\frac{D-4}{2}R/\nu$ and the mass, area and spin diverge; in $D=4$ the mass and $S_\nu$ stay finite (an AdS embedding suggests why), but $r_h$ still diverges.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p5">At fixed mass the horizon also diverges and, for $D>4$, the temperature goes to infinity, beyond the EFT.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p6">Extrapolating both saddles to $R\sim l_s$: the black hole has $M,S_\nu\sim\nu^{4-D}$, $r_h\sim l_s/\nu$ ($D>4$), the W star $M,S_\nu\sim\nu^{-1/2}$, $L\sim l_s/\sqrt\nu$; both diverge, agreeing up to the power for $D>4$, while $D=4$ is more elusive.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-bh_w_trans-p7">So both saddles decouple at the index, each growing large and heavy; the W star's limit resembles its low-temperature limit, where it arguably becomes a stringy black hole.</span></p>""",
    "sec-charged_saddles": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-charged_setup">With momentum and winding on an extra circle the index is non-trivial, $S_\text{micro}=4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}$, and an $O(2,2;\mathbb R)$ transformation fixing the winding charge vector maps any neutral saddle to a charged one, with an $\alpha'$-exact thermodynamic map for arbitrary $n$ and $\nu$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh">For weakly curved black holes the map reproduces the two-charge black hole; Sen's thermal limit gives the right charge dependence with zero area, and near the index a second Sen-like limit gives non-zero area but zero index with singularities, while reliable black holes need $T\gg T_*=1/(G_N|Q|\nu^{D-4})$ and move away from BPS as $\nu\to0$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars">The charged W star's entropy approaches $S_\text{micro}=2\pi\sqrt{Q_R^2-Q_L^2}$, yet its reliable window between the transitions to black holes and to free strings disappears as $\nu\to0$; its size at the free-string transition, $L^2\sim\sqrt{Q_nQ_w}$, is that of a BPS string, suggesting a smooth transition to a BPS string phase.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-charged_setup": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-charged_setup-p1">Fixing momentum and winding $Q_n,Q_w$ on a circle of circumference $2\pi r$ makes the index non-trivial, with the free-string value $S_\text{micro}=4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}$; charged solutions are generated from the neutral ones.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_setup-p2">The classical $O(2,2;\mathbb R)$ acting on $S^1_R\times S^1_r$ is chosen to fix the winding-mode charge vector, so it depends on the thermal momentum $n(\nu)$, which is $0$ for bosonic and type II and $(2-2\nu+\nu^2)/2$ for heterotic.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_setup-p3">Equating the tree-level partition functions of seed and image and differentiating gives $R$, $r$, $Q_{L,R}$, $S_\nu$ and $M$ in terms of the seed and two boosts $\upalpha,\upbeta$ (4.5), with the dilaton rescaled by $R/\tilde R$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_setup-p4">The map is $\alpha'$-exact and generalises Chen–Maldacena–Witten to any $n$ and $\nu$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-charged_bh": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh-p1">For a weakly curved seed the map simplifies to (4.9), the $n$ dependence disappears, and one recovers the two-charge black hole.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh-p2 subsec-charged_bh-p3">Sen's scaled limit $\upbeta\to\infty$, $\tilde M'\to0$ gives a BPS solution with $S=\pi\tilde R\sqrt{Q_R^2-Q_L^2}$, the right charge dependence but unreliable at $\tilde M'\sim1$ and with vanishing area in GR, the small black hole; whether $\alpha'$ corrections resolve it has been argued both ways, and alternatively the charged hole may transition to a charged Horowitz–Polchinski solution.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh-p4 subsec-charged_bh-p5">At small $\nu$ there are two kinds of solutions; the slightly transformed one has a diverging seed mass, so its mass and size diverge and it decouples like the neutral hole.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh-p6">The large-$\upbeta$ one implements a Sen-like limit with $M-|Q_R|\sim e^{-2\upbeta}$, non-vanishing area but vanishing index, and curvature singularities at $\nu=0$ that recent proposals resolve with $\alpha'$ corrections; the paper stays agnostic and keeps to weakly curved holes.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_bh-p7">Those exist only for $T\gg T_*=1/(G_N|Q|\nu^{D-4})$; at fixed charges and small $\nu$ they are far from BPS and eventually decouple.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-charged_w_stars": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars-p1">Using the W star's equation of state $\tilde S_\nu'=2\pi R_H\tilde M'$ in the map gives $S_\nu=\pi(R_H-n/R_H)\sqrt{M^2-Q_R^2}+\pi(R_H+n/R_H)\sqrt{M^2-Q_L^2}$, a Cardy-like formula valid for any winding condensate; near the heterotic index the right-moving part vanishes and $S_\nu=2\pi\sqrt{M^2-Q_L^2}+O(\sqrt\nu)$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars-p2 subsec-charged_w_stars-p3">A slightly transformed W star decouples like the neutral one, while the large-$\upbeta$ one saturates the BPS bound with $S_\text{index}\approx2\pi\sqrt{Q_R^2-Q_L^2}=S_\text{micro}$, approached as $e^{-2\upbeta}$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars-p4 subsec-charged_w_stars-p5">The Sen limit is still unreliable: the W star turns into a black hole below $T_\text{trans}\sim1/(g_s^2|Q|\sqrt\nu)$ and into free strings beyond $T_\text{gas}\sim1/(g_s^2|Q|^{(6-d)/2}\nu)$, so it is reliable only in between, and no W star exactly saturates BPS.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars-p6">As $\nu\to0$, $T_\text{trans}$, $T_\text{gas}$ and $T_*$ all diverge, so the charged W star decouples too.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-charged_w_stars-p7 subsec-charged_w_stars-p8">At $\nu\to0$ the seed mass goes to zero and the W star is transitioning to free strings, with size $L^2\sim\sqrt{Q_nQ_w}$, that of a generic BPS string, so the transition to a BPS string phase appears smooth; either only BPS strings survive the limit, or the W star is not connected to the black hole, which becomes the small black hole instead.</span></p>""",
    "sec-type_ii": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-type_ii-p1 subsec-ii_worldsheet">Near the index the physics depends on the string theory; for type II the winding ground state has $m^2=R^2-2\nu$, vanishing at $R=0$ rather than at a self-dual radius, so for small $\nu$ every winding sector up to $w\sim1/\sqrt\nu$ is light.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_eft_etc">Too many light fields for a $d$-dimensional EFT, but in the T-dual picture they are momentum modes on a large circle, and the $(d+1)$-dimensional type II supergravity on the T-dual Melvin background, with the tachyonic modes of David, Gutperle, Headrick and Minwalla, is the effective theory; in the $m^2\ll\nu$ limit one mode condenses and a string star with the W star's properties is expected.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_charged">The charged entropy $S_\nu=\pi\sqrt{2\nu}(\sqrt{M^2-Q_R^2}+\sqrt{M^2-Q_L^2})$ seems to vanish at the index; the free-string partition function shows that $\nu\to0$ and $M\to\infty$ do not commute, and a generic string crosses over from Hagedorn to BPS behaviour for $M\ll1/\nu$, so a smooth transition is still plausible.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-ii_worldsheet": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-ii_worldsheet-p1">The type II mass-shell condition gives the $w=1$ ground state $m^2=R^2-2\nu$: the known $R_H=\sqrt2$ at $\nu=1$, and at the index the classical winding energy $m^2=R^2$, non-negative and vanishing at $R=0$, so the light-winding regime is $R\ll1$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_worldsheet-p2">Consequently many sectors are light at once: for small $\nu$ the $w$ ground state has $m_w^2=w^2R^2-2\nu w$, tachyonic for $R^2<2\nu/w$ and light for $w\ll1/\sqrt\nu$.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-ii_eft_etc": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-ii_eft_etc-p1 subsec-ii_eft_etc-p2">An EFT with $\sim1/\sqrt\nu$ light fields cannot be written in $d$ dimensions, but after T-duality the windings are momentum modes on a large circle $R'=1/R$ with $m^2(p)=p^2-2q|p|$, $q=\nu/R$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_eft_etc-p3">The T-dual of the Melvin background is weakly coupled and weakly curved for $q\ll1$, and the light modes are the metric, $B$-field and dilaton fluctuations (5.11), Gaussian around the axis.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_eft_etc-p4">In the limit $R-R_H(\nu)\ll\sqrt\nu$ only the $p=1/R'$ mode is light; integrating out the long-range forces should give a $(d-2)$-dimensional theory of the form (3.16), so type II string stars exist for small $\nu$ and the comparison with black holes carries over.</span></p>""",
    "subsec-ii_charged": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsec-ii_charged-p1">The charged type II string star has $S_\nu=\pi\sqrt{2\nu}(\sqrt{M^2-Q_R^2}+\sqrt{M^2-Q_L^2})$, which matches the free-string index at $\nu=1$ but seems to give $S_\text{index}=0$ at $\nu=0$.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_charged-p2">The right-moving free-string partition function grows as $e^{\nu/B}$ at fixed $\nu$ but as $e^{2/B}$ at $\nu=0$: the limits do not commute, generic states at fixed $\nu$ carry both chiralities and are far from BPS, while for $1\ll M\ll1/\nu$ they are approximately BPS.</span> <span data-covers="subsec-ii_charged-p3 subsec-ii_charged-p4">So the string star plausibly transitions smoothly to free strings, which approach the BPS entropy at lower masses, leaving only a BPS string phase at $\nu=0$; the obstruction is specific to type II, echoing the worldsheet-index disagreement of [12].</span></p>""",
    "sec-bosonic": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-bosonic-p1">Bosonic strings have no index and $\omega=-i\beta\Omega$ has period $2\pi$; the winding ground state is unique and continuous in $\omega$, so the string star goes to itself under $\omega\mapsto\omega+2\pi$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-bosonic-p2">Individual black holes are not periodic, and summing the branches makes the dominant action $I_\text{BH}(\omega)$ have a derivative discontinuity at $\omega=\pi$, which the string star lacks.</span> <span data-covers="sec-bosonic-p3 sec-bosonic-p4">If the saddles are continuously related at small $\omega$, the discontinuity must arise at an intermediate temperature and be resolved by tree-level stringy effects; the same tension exists in AdS$_3$ with its $SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ family of black holes.</span></p>""",
    "sec-discussion": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-discussion-p1 sec-discussion-p2">Near the index the analysis depends on the string theory: heterotic strings are described by a Higgsed $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$ supergravity whose W boson is the winding mode, with the Hagedorn instability as Ambjørn–Olesen condensation and a string star that decouples at $\nu=0$ like the black hole; type II strings need a T-dual dimension and still admit a string star in a low-energy limit.</span> <span data-covers="sec-discussion-p3 sec-discussion-p4">Charged string stars interpolate between free strings and black holes for $0<\nu\ll1$ and decouple at $\nu=0$, leaving a BPS string phase, in tension with the proposed small black hole; the heterotic transition looks smooth, the type II one needs a Hagedorn-to-BPS crossover.</span> <span data-covers="sec-discussion-p5 sec-discussion-p6">Extensions include intermediate temperatures, worldsheet Wilson coefficients, several angular velocities and thermal Wilson lines; for bosonic strings the black hole has a first-order transition at $\omega=\pi$ that the string star does not, an issue that fermionic theories postpone to $\omega=2\pi$.</span></p>""",
    "app-sugra": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-sugra-p1 app-sugra-p2">Starting from the bosonic action of 9d half-maximal gauged supergravity with scalar coset $O(k+1,1)/O(k+1)$, the $k=0$ case reproduces the circle reduction (2.19).</span> <span data-covers="app-sugra-p3 app-sugra-p4 app-sugra-p5">With $k=2$ the scalars are a Lorentz boost of rapidity $S_\alpha$ and the gauging is $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_R$; working out the gauge and scalar kinetic terms gives the action (A.13) with vanishing potential, consistent with the exact worldsheet modulus.</span> <span data-covers="app-sugra-p6">The unitary-gauge constraint (A.16) reduces to the Lorentz condition around the supersymmetric background.</span></p>""",
    "app-thermal_eft": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-thermal_eft-p1 app-thermal_eft-p2">With $\hat\nu=1-\nu$, the Atick–Witten thermal scalar has the quadratic action (B.2), charged under the KK vectors, with theory-dependent constant $c$ and thermal momentum $n$.</span> <span data-covers="app-thermal_eft-p3">On the Melvin background its spectrum is the Landau levels (B.7), reproducing the worldsheet near the thermal point.</span></p>""",
    "app-adjoint_higgs": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-adjoint_higgs-p1 app-adjoint_higgs-p2 app-adjoint_higgs-p3">A W boson in a constant magnetic field, with the quadratic action (C.1) and its gauge constraint; transverse polarisations behave as a charged scalar.</span> <span data-covers="app-adjoint_higgs-p4">Landau levels from two oscillator pairs give eigenvalues independent of $k$, the familiar degeneracy.</span> <span data-covers="app-adjoint_higgs-p5 app-adjoint_higgs-p6">Longitudinal modes mix with a pure-gauge mode; after diagonalisation the Zeeman shift $\pm2B$ makes $w_-$ tachyonic for $B>m^2$, the Ambjørn–Olesen condensation.</span></p>""",
    "app-sol_gen": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-sol_gen-p1">The $O(2,2;\mathbb R)$ generators that fix the winding vector form an $sl(2)$; exponentiating them gives the transformed asymptotic metric, $B$-field and dilaton (D.7–D.8).</span> <span data-covers="app-sol_gen-p2">Differentiating the equality of partition functions gives the charges, entropy and mass of the image, and the variables $\upalpha,\upbeta$ bring the map to the form (4.5) used in the text.</span></p>""",
}
